\subsection{Projection and Unfolding Proof Sketches}
\label{subsec:proof_sketches}

Let $\pi_{Ri}(\tilde{e}, q_\theta, S, T_s, T_g) = \frac{g\,\theta_z(T_s,\tilde{e})}{\theta_0 S^2}$ denote the scalar Richardson-number projection mapping. Its differential is the non-zero row functional:
\begin{equation}
D\pi_{Ri} = \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{g}{\theta_0 S^2}\frac{\partial \theta_z}{\partial \tilde{e}} & 0 & -\frac{2g\theta_z}{\theta_0 S^3} & \frac{g}{\theta_0 S^2}\frac{\partial \theta_z}{\partial T_s} & 0
\end{pmatrix}.
\label{eq:D_pi_Ri}
\end{equation}
Thus $\operatorname{rank}(D\pi_{Ri}) = 1$ whenever $S > 0$ and $\theta_z > 0$. Because the fold surface $\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{fold}} \subset \mathbb{R}^5$ is three-dimensional, counting tangent space dimensions yields
\begin{equation}
\dim\bigl(T_p\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{fold}} \cap \ker D\pi_{Ri}(p)\bigr) \ge 3 + 4 - 5 = 2.
\label{eq:kernel_dimension}
\end{equation}
Equation \eqref{eq:kernel_dimension} proves that the kernel of the restricted differential map $D\pi_{Ri}|_{T_p\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{fold}}}$ is non-trivial. Consequently, scalar $Ri$ level sets intersect the folded fast manifold along two-dimensional fibers rather than at single points—establishing the mathematical proof that $Ri_{\text{crit}}$ is a dynamic geometric projection rather than a static fluid constant (Theorem 3).

For the Cusp catastrophe unfolding, let $(\eta_1, \eta_2)$ be center-manifold control coordinates induced by geostrophic shear and deep-soil temperature. The physical unfolding matrix is
\begin{equation}
M = \begin{pmatrix}
\partial_{S_g} \eta_1 & \partial_{T_{\mathrm{deep}}} \eta_1 \\[0.4em]
\partial_{S_g} \eta_2 & \partial_{T_{\mathrm{deep}}} \eta_2
\end{pmatrix},
\label{eq:unfolding_matrix}
\end{equation}
with leading diagonal entries $\partial_{S_g}\eta_1 = \epsilon_1 / (\tilde{e}\tau_S)$ and $\partial_{T_{\mathrm{deep}}}\eta_2 = \epsilon_1\epsilon_2 k_g / (\tilde{e} C_s d_g)$. The determinant evaluates to
\begin{equation}
\det(M) = \frac{\epsilon_1^2\epsilon_2 k_g}{\tilde{e}^2 C_s d_g \tau_S}\left(1 - \frac{\partial R_{\mathrm{net}}}{\partial T_s}\frac{d_g}{k_g}\right).
\label{eq:det_M}
\end{equation}
Because linearized nocturnal thermal radiation satisfies $\partial R_{\mathrm{net}}/\partial T_s < 0$, the bracketed term in \eqref{eq:det_M} is strictly greater than 1, ensuring $\det(M) > 0$. The Cusp unfolding is therefore transverse, structurally stable, and non-degenerate in the $(S_g, T_{\mathrm{deep}})$ parameter space.